---
// src/pages/events.astro - the events calendar.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THERE IS NO <noscript> FALLBACK, CORRECTING AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS FILE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The first version of this page carried a <noscript> block in every row - a
// "Details for X" anchor - plus a page-level <noscript> telling the reader to
// use those links when JavaScript is off. Both are gone, and the reason matters
// more than the removal:
//
//   THE PREMISE WAS FALSE. The original comment said "without scripting the
//   radios do nothing useful". Radios are not scripting. `:checked` is a CSS
//   pseudo-class and `:has()` is a CSS selector, so the whole selection
//   mechanism works with scripting disabled - which was the entire design goal.
//
//   AND THE FALLBACK WAS DEAD ANYWAY. The anchor pointed at the event's panel
//   element, and that panel is `display: none` unless its radio is `:checked`.
//   There was no `:target` rule anywhere, so the link scrolled to a hidden
//   element and did nothing. It was a dead control described in a comment as
//   "a link that works, not a disabled control".
//
// A review caught both. So there is no fallback, and instead the real
// dependency is stated plainly below: this page needs `:has()`, which is
// Baseline from Chrome 105, Safari 15.4 and Firefox 121 (late 2022 / early
// 2023). That is OLDER than `light-dark()`, which src/styles/themes.css already
// depends on for the theme system, so it is not a new floor for this site - and
// a browser too old for it could not have rendered the themes either.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE INTERACTION, AND WHY THERE IS NO SCRIPT ON THIS PAGE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The list of events is a radio group (see src/components/EventRow.astro for why
// a radio rather than buttons). Selecting one reveals that event's panel - its
// large banner image, its description and its notes - beside the list on a wide
// screen, and above the list on a phone.
//
// Every panel for every event is rendered into the page, and exactly one is
// visible. Visibility is decided by CSS alone:
//
//     html:has(#event-input-<id>:checked) [data-panel='<id>'] { ... shown ... }
//
// with the default rule hiding all of them. That is the same `:has()` mechanism
// the theme switcher already uses in src/styles/themes.css, for the same reason:
// a control that repaints through CSS cannot flash, cannot desync from its own
// state, and keeps working with scripting off.
//
// Rendering N panels rather than swapping one is the deliberate trade. The cost
// is markup proportional to the number of events. The benefit is that the whole
// feature has no moving parts: no state, no handler, nothing to keep in sync,
// and it behaves identically with the script blocked. A page of forty events is
// a few tens of kilobytes of HTML, which is not a cost worth optimising away for
// a mechanism that cannot drift.
//
// Only ONE `checked` radio is rendered into the markup - the first upcoming event
// - so there is always a panel showing on first paint. Selecting a different
// radio moves the `:checked` state and the CSS follows. A reader with scripting
// off gets the <noscript> anchor on each row instead, which jumps to the same
// panel.
//
// WHY NO FILTERING BY TYPE HERE, DESPITE TYPE BEING A FIELD. A type filter needs
// to know which rows to show, and that means either scripting or URL state. Both
// are out of scope for a page whose whole point is to work without either. The
// type is rendered on every row and as a chip in the panel, and the calendar is
// short enough to read. The schema already derives the set of types present from
// the data, so a filter is a later change to this page, not a new data contract.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

import PageShell from '../components/PageShell.astro'
import EventRow from '../components/EventRow.astro'
import {
  eventCount,
  events,
  formatEventDate,
  formatEventTime,
  formatEventZone,
  groupByMonth,
  isPast,
  machineDateTime,
  reviewedOn,
  sortEvents
} from '../lib/events'
import { withBase } from '../lib/site'

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The split, decided BY THE DATA and not by the clock
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// See `reviewedOn` in src/lib/events-schema.ts. Calling new Date() here would make
// the built HTML depend on the day the build ran, which is ROADMAP design rule 8
// broken in the most literal way available: commit once, build on two different
// days, get two different pages. The date a human last reviewed the list is in
// the file, so the split is reproducible and the reader can be told when it was
// last checked.

const ordered = sortEvents(events)
const past = reviewedOn === null ? [] : ordered.filter((event) => isPast(event, reviewedOn))
const upcoming = reviewedOn === null ? ordered : ordered.filter((event) => !isPast(event, reviewedOn))

// The first UPCOMING event is the one shown on first paint. When there is no
// upcoming event the first of whatever exists is, because a calendar whose panel
// is empty on arrival is a page that has not rendered.
const initial = upcoming[0] ?? ordered[0]

// Grouped only for the upcoming list. The past list is short by construction and
// reads better as one flat run under a single heading than as four near-empty
// month headings.
const upcomingGroups = groupByMonth(upcoming)

// The headline states the real range rather than implying permanence. A calendar
// of dated events that says "upcoming events" with no window is the same class of
// claim the reading room refuses to make about a 20-item feed.
const firstDate = ordered.length > 0 ? formatEventDate(ordered[0].startsAt) : '';
const lastDate = ordered.length > 0 ? formatEventDate(ordered[ordered.length - 1].startsAt) : '';

// EVERY CLAIM IN HERE IS DERIVED. The first version of this sentence said "Each
// row carries a thumbnail; select one to see its details and banner", and a
// review checked it against the data: `election-day` has no thumbnail, and both
// it and `winter-open-lecture` have no banner, so three of seven rows
// contradicted the page's own metadata. That is the exact failure this project
// exists to prevent, expressed in prose instead of in a field - so the sentence
// now says only what is true of every row.
const description =
  `The events calendar: ${eventCount} dated events` +
  (firstDate && lastDate ? ` between ${firstDate} and ${lastDate}` : '') +
  ', grouped by month and ordered soonest first. Select an event to see its details.'
---

<PageShell title="Events" description={description}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-head__title">Events</h1>

    <p class="page-head__note">
      This calendar is written by hand and reviewed rather than generated, so it says
      exactly what is on it and no more.
      {
        reviewedOn && (
          <span class="page-head__reviewed">
            {' '}
            Last reviewed <strong>{formatEventDate(reviewedOn)}</strong>. Dates are shown in
            each venue's own local time.
          </span>
        )
      }
    </p>
  </div>

  {/*
    NO EMPTY STATE, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE COUPLING.

    An earlier version wrapped this in `eventCount === 0 ? <empty state> : ...`.
    That branch was UNREACHABLE: validateEvents rejects an empty `events` array
    with reason `events-empty`, src/lib/events.ts throws on any rejection, and so
    this page never rendered with zero events. A guard for a state the schema
    forbids is the "written but never read" shape - it reads as a supported case
    and documents one that cannot occur.

    The rule that replaces it lives in the schema, where it belongs: an events
    page with no events is a page claiming to have a calendar, so the honest
    response is to fail the build and delete the page rather than render an empty
    one. If that rule is ever relaxed, THIS TEMPLATE MUST GAIN AN EMPTY STATE -
    the two are coupled and the coupling is deliberate.
  */}
  <div class="calendar">
        {/*
          THE PANEL. One region, one announced name, and one panel per event
          inside it - only one of which is visible at a time.

          aria-live="polite" is what makes the panel usable rather than merely
          correct: selecting a radio moves the :checked state, and CSS alone
          cannot tell a screen reader that the region below now describes a
          different event. Without it the selection is silent.

          The comment about not setting aria-atomic is a real constraint, not an
          omission: with atomic set, every selection would re-announce the whole
          region including the banner's alt text, which is long and is repeated
          from the row's own name. Polite plus non-atomic announces the change.
        */}
        <aside
          class="calendar__panel"
          id="event-panel"
          aria-label="Highlighted event"
          aria-live="polite"
        >
          {
            ordered.map((event) => (
              <article
                class="panel"
                id={`event-${event.id}`}
                data-panel={event.id}
                aria-labelledby={`event-name-${event.id}`}
              >
                {event.banner ? (
                  <div class="panel__banner">
                    <img
                      class="panel__image"
                      src={withBase(event.banner)}
                      alt=""
                      loading="lazy"
                      decoding="async"
                      width="1600"
                      height="600"
                    />
                  </div>
                ) : (
                  <div class="panel__banner panel__banner--absent">
                    <p class="panel__banner-absent">
                      No banner image has been supplied for this event.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                )}

                <p class="panel__meta">
                  <span class="chip">{event.type}</span>
                  <time class="panel__when" datetime={machineDateTime(event.startsAt)}>
                    {formatEventDate(event.startsAt)}
                    {formatEventTime(event.startsAt, event.endsAt) && (
                      <span class="panel__time">
                        {', '}
                        {formatEventTime(event.startsAt, event.endsAt)}
                      </span>
                    )}
                  </time>
                  {formatEventZone(event.timezone) && (
                    <span class="panel__zone">{formatEventZone(event.timezone)}</span>
                  )}
                </p>

                <h2 class="panel__name" id={`event-name-${event.id}`}>
                  {event.name}
                </h2>

                <p class="panel__event">{event.event}</p>

                <p class="panel__location">
                  <span class="label panel__location-label">Where</span>
                  <span class="panel__location-text">{event.location}</span>
                </p>

                {event.notes && <p class="panel__notes">{event.notes}</p>}

                {event.url && (
                  <p class="panel__link">
                    <a
                      class="panel__anchor"
                      href={event.url}
                      rel="external noopener noreferrer"
                      target="_blank"
                    >
                      <span>Event details</span>
                      <span class="panel__link-marker" aria-hidden="true">
                        &#8599;
                      </span>
                      <span class="visually-hidden">(external link, opens in a new tab)</span>
                    </a>
                  </p>
                )}

                {/*
                  The return link is INSIDE the panel, so it is subject to the same
                  visibility rule as the rest of it and cannot be focused while
                  hidden. A hidden-but-focusable link is the classic keyboard trap,
                  and adding tabindex="-1" would only hide the trap from the very
                  screen reader most likely to land in it.
                */}
                <p class="panel__back">
                  <a class="panel__back-link" href="#events-list">
                    Back to the list
                  </a>
                </p>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </aside>

        {/*
          id="events-list" is the target of the "Back to the list" link in every
          panel. It has to be a real element with that id, not a scroll-margin
          hack: an anchor whose target does not exist does nothing at all, and it
          would be a dead control that looks fine.
        */}
        <div class="calendar__list" id="events-list">
          {
            past.length > 0 && (
              <section class="events-section events-section--past" aria-labelledby="events-past">
                <h2 class="events-section__title" id="events-past">
                  Already held
                </h2>
                <ul class="events__list">
                  {past.map((event) => (
                    <EventRow
                      event={event}
                      selected={initial !== undefined && initial.id === event.id}
                      past
                    />
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </section>
            )
          }

          {
            upcoming.length > 0 ? (
              <section class="events-section" aria-labelledby="events-upcoming">
                <h2 class="events-section__title" id="events-upcoming">
                  Upcoming
                </h2>
                {upcomingGroups.map((group) => (
                  <div class="events-month">
                    <h3 class="events-month__title">{group.label}</h3>
                    <ul class="events__list">
                      {group.events.map((event) => (
                        <EventRow
                          event={event}
                          selected={initial !== undefined && initial.id === event.id}
                          past={false}
                        />
                      ))}
                    </ul>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </section>
            ) : (
              past.length > 0 && (
                <p class="events-none">
                  Nothing upcoming is listed at the moment. Past events are above, and
                  new dates appear here as they are confirmed.
                </p>
              )
            )
          }
        </div>
      </div>

  {/*
    Stated as ordinary prose rather than in a <noscript>, because it is not a
    scripting caveat: the calendar's selection works with scripting OFF, being
    pure CSS. The one real dependency is `:has()` support, and it is older than
    the `light-dark()` that src/styles/themes.css already relies on, so a browser
    too old for this page could not have rendered the themes either. See the
    header comment.
  */}
  <p class="events-note">
    Selecting an event swaps the banner without reloading the page, and works with
    scripting switched off. Use the arrow keys to move between events.
  </p>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .page-head {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-5);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-5);
    border-block-end: var(--rule-width) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .page-head__title {
    font-size: var(--size-3xl);
  }

  .page-head__note {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-4);
    border-inline-start: var(--rule-width-strong) solid var(--rule-strong);
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .page-head__reviewed {
    display: block;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   * THE LAYOUT
   *
   * One column by default. Above 64rem it becomes list + panel side by side, and
   * the panel is sticky so it stays beside the list as the reader scrolls rather
   * than being stranded at the top of a long column.
   *
   * `order: -1` on the panel puts it FIRST on a phone. That is the answer to
   * "or below on a phone", chosen over a panel below the list for one reason: a
   * reader who taps an event near the top of a long list gets no visible feedback
   * at all if the panel is off the bottom of the screen, and the tap reads as
   * broken. Above the list, and stuck to the top of the viewport, the selection
   * is always in view. It costs vertical space, and the banner is cropped short
   * on a narrow screen to keep that cost small.
   * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .calendar {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-5);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .calendar__panel {
    order: -1;
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--space-2);
    z-index: 1;
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .calendar {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 24rem);
      gap: var(--space-6);
    }

    /*
     * BOTH ITEMS ARE PINNED TO ROW 1, and that is a bug fix, not a style choice.
     *
     * The panel comes FIRST in the DOM (so it stacks above the list on a phone and
     * so a screen reader reaches the detail before the list of choices), and it is
     * assigned `grid-column: 2`. With the default `grid-auto-flow: row`, that is
     * enough on its own to make auto-placement put the panel in row 1 and push the
     * list into row 2 column 1 - so the two ended up DIAGONAL, with a large empty
     * block filling the left of the panel's row and the list stranded far below it.
     *
     * A browser check found this, not a test: node --test cannot see layout, and
     * every assertion in test/events.test.mjs passed against a page whose entire
     * desktop arrangement was wrong. Stating `grid-row: 1` on both items is what
     * actually makes "beside" mean beside.
     *
     * `align-items: start` on .calendar is what makes the sticky panel work: it
     * keeps the panel at its natural height inside a row as tall as the list, so
     * sticky has room to travel down the column instead of being pinned to a row
     * exactly its own height.
     *
     * TAB ORDER, AND THE TRADE THIS MAKES. The panel is FIRST in the DOM, so on a
     * phone - where it is also visually first - DOM order and visual order agree
     * exactly. On a wide screen the panel is visually second, so a keyboard reader
     * reaches the panel's two or three links before the radio group. That is
     * accepted rather than engineered away, because the alternatives are worse: the
     * radio group is the primary control on this page and one Tab press should
     * reach it, and the panel is sticky and on screen at the top right, so it is
     * not a spatial surprise to arrive there first. Reversing the DOM order would
     * simply move the mismatch to the phone breakpoint, where it would be worse -
     * there the panel sits directly above the reader's thumb.
     */
    .calendar__panel {
      order: 0;
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1;
      top: var(--space-4);
    }

    .calendar__list {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   * SELECTION
   *
   * Both halves are needed and neither is enough alone. The `display: none`
   * default is what removes the other N-1 panels from the accessibility tree and
   * from tab order; the `:has()` rule is what shows the selected one. Without
   * the default, every panel is visible and every panel's links are focusable.
   *
   * The `html:has(...)` selectors are written out longhand rather than generated,
   * so that what the browser matches is readable in DevTools by a human who has
   * never seen this file.
   * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .panel {
    display: none;
  }

  html:has(#event-input-county-budget-debate:checked) [data-panel='county-budget-debate'],
  html:has(#event-input-harvest-dinner-fundraiser:checked) [data-panel='harvest-dinner-fundraiser'],
  html:has(#event-input-election-day:checked) [data-panel='election-day'],
  html:has(#event-input-school-board-rally:checked) [data-panel='school-board-rally'],
  html:has(#event-input-riverside-water-forum:checked) [data-panel='riverside-water-forum'],
  html:has(#event-input-records-workshop:checked) [data-panel='records-workshop'],
  html:has(#event-input-winter-open-lecture:checked) [data-panel='winter-open-lecture'] {
    display: block;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   * THE PANEL
   * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .panel__banner {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 7;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--chip-bg);
    border: var(--rule-width) solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  .panel__image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /*
   * THE ABSENT-BANNER SLOT.
   *
   * A panel whose event has no banner keeps the same frame and the same height
   * as one that does, and says so in words inside it. The alternative - collapsing
   * the slot - was rejected for a specific reason rather than on taste: on a phone
   * the panel is sticky at the top of the viewport, and a panel that changes height
   * when the reader selects a different event makes the content underneath jump
   * under their finger. A stable frame also means the layout does not depend on
   * which images happen to exist in the dataset, so the page cannot be reshaped by
   * a data change.
   */
  .panel__banner--absent {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    background: var(--chip-bg);
  }

  .panel__banner-absent {
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline: var(--space-4);
    max-width: none;
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .panel__meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .panel__meta > * + *::before {
    content: '\00b7';
    margin-inline-end: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .panel__zone {
    font-size: var(--size-xs);
  }

  .panel__name {
    font-size: var(--size-xl);
  }

  .panel__event {
    font-size: var(--size-md);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
  }

  .panel__location {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-3);
    border-block-start: var(--rule-width) solid var(--rule);
  }

  .panel__location-text {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
  }

  .panel__notes {
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .panel__link {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .panel__anchor {
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
  }

  /* A shape, not a colour: the outbound signal has to survive greyscale printing
     and any form of colour vision deficiency. Same convention as ArticleCard. */
  .panel__link-marker {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-inline-start: var(--space-1);
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .panel__back {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }

  .panel__back-link {
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   * THE LIST
   * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
  .events-section {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-6);
  }

  .events-section__title {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--size-lg);
  }

  .events-month__title {
    margin-block: var(--space-5) var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--size-md);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caps);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .events__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin-block-end: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 0;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   * THE SELECTED ROW IS NOT STYLED HERE, AND THAT IS CORRECT
   *
   * The accent rule and the focus ring for a selected row live in
   * EventRow.astro's scoped block, because the scope id Astro appends to a
   * selector is per COMPONENT: a rule in this file can only match elements this
   * file renders, and `.row` is rendered by EventRow. A review caught the
   * obvious version of this - `:global(.events__item:has(...)) .row` written
   * here - compiling to a selector whose scope id matched nothing at all, which
   * silently removed the focus ring from the entire page. See the long note in
   * EventRow.astro.
   *
   * `:global()` un-scopes only what is to its LEFT; Astro still appends the
   * page's scope id to the last compound. That is the trap, and it fails
   * silently.
   *
   * NOTHING in this block may name `.row`, `.events__item` or `.events__radio`.
   * test/events.test.mjs fails the suite if one does, because no assertion in it
   * can see a scope id.
   * --------------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .events-none,
  .events-note {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--size-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .events-note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-4);
    border-block-start: var(--rule-width) solid var(--rule);
  }

  /* The `.events-empty` rules that used to live here were deleted with the empty
     state they styled. They were the same kind of dead code as the markup: a
     branch the schema makes unreachable, still carrying CSS. See the note in the
     markup about the deliberate coupling. */
</style>
